\begin{frontmatter}

\title{Which representation clusters? A chance-corrected benchmark of ECG
representations for unsupervised arrhythmia structure}

\author[uh]{Dongho Chun}
\author[af]{Axel Faes\corref{cor1}}
\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author.}
\ead{axel.faes@uhasselt.be}
\affiliation[uh]{organization={Data Science Institute (DSI), Hasselt University}, city={Hasselt}, country={Belgium}}
\affiliation[af]{organization={Biomedical Data Sciences, Data Science Institute (DSI) and Biomedical Research Institute (BIOMED), Hasselt University}, city={Hasselt}, country={Belgium}}

\begin{abstract}
Unsupervised analysis of the electrocardiogram (ECG) aims to find cardiac structure without labels. Whether clustering succeeds depends on the algorithm and on how the signal is represented, and this study measures the effect of the representation. We compare five representations for the unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from atrial fibrillation (AF) on PTB-XL. They are raw flattening of the whole recording, a convolutional autoencoder, 16 hand-crafted rhythm and morphology features, and the frozen embeddings of two foundation models, HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder. The evaluation protocol reads each chance-corrected clustering score against the cluster sizes and against a supervised ceiling, computed with patient-grouped cross-validation on the same records and representation. It also sweeps the feature conditioner and repeats each seeded clustering fit over ten seeds. ECGFounder clustered best (adjusted Rand index, ARI, 0.885), and the hand-crafted features reached 0.711. Raw flattening and the autoencoder scored at chance (ARI near 0), although the autoencoder's supervised ceiling (0.678) lay above the majority-class proportion (0.504). On a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task the hand-crafted beat features reached an ARI of 0.783. The silhouette score gave 0.81 to a solution with one dominant cluster and an ARI of 0. The mean ARI of the hand-crafted features fell from 0.63 under standard scaling to 0.23 under quantile scaling. At a 9:1 SR:AF ratio the hand-crafted features reached an ARI of 0.232, against 0.060 for ECGFounder. In a simulated federation of ten sites with skewed class proportions, ECGFounder fell from 0.885 to 0.612 and the hand-crafted features from 0.644 to 0.591. These findings hold for two binary tasks, five clustering algorithms, two named foundation models and a simulated federation.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
electrocardiography \sep unsupervised clustering \sep representation learning \sep
foundation models \sep adjusted Rand index \sep PTB-XL
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Most machine-learning work on the electrocardiogram (ECG) is supervised. Several clinically interesting questions are unsupervised, however: which patients group together, what subtypes exist, and whether structure can be found without labels that are expensive or unavailable. A natural first attempt is to cluster recordings directly. Whether that attempt succeeds is usually attributed to the clustering algorithm. The representation fed to the algorithm may matter more, and this study measures how much.

This study began as a re-examination of a master's project that clustered whole
12-lead recordings. That pipeline flattened each strip into a long vector, compressed
it with a dense autoencoder, and clustered the code. It reported high cluster purity
and a silhouette score near 0.89, but the purity simply equalled the majority-class
proportion in every setting, and the silhouette was measured on a degenerate solution
in which almost all points fell into a single cluster. The honest conclusion of that
work, that the difficulty lies in processing the raw signal rather than in the
clustering step, is correct and is the starting point here. We make it precise.

A representation can carry information about a label and still fail to cluster by it. Linear separability and clusterability are distinct properties: in self-supervised image models, representations with nearly the same linear-probe accuracy can differ in clustering quality \cite{sslclustering2025}. We read such a gap as a representation placing the discriminative direction away from its dominant axes of variation. A supervised probe then recovers the label while an unsupervised partition does not. This reading is ours, and we treat the distinction itself as background rather than as a finding. What has not been measured, for ECG, is how the common representation families compare on this axis under chance-corrected metrics.

The most directly related work, by Filice et al.~\cite{lookingbeyond2026}, benchmarks ECG foundation models against one another on four 12-lead datasets, PTB-XL among them. Its representation-wise evaluation includes the adjusted Rand index (ARI) of Gaussian-mixture clusters of the frozen embeddings. The ARI measures the agreement between clusters and labels, corrected for chance. The authors state that earlier benchmarks lack a representation-wise evaluation. Their benchmark includes no hand-crafted features and does not use the MIT-BIH database. Hand-crafted features measure named physiological quantities, such as RR intervals and P-wave presence, and need no trained network. MIT-BIH is the canonical morphology benchmark. This study adds both.

We therefore compare five representations for the unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from atrial fibrillation (AF), with the controls the original project lacked. Because SR and AF differ by definition in the regularity of the rhythm, we expected representations that encode rhythm to recover the split. The contribution of the study is an evaluation protocol for label-free ECG structure, applied to these representations. It has four parts:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Each chance-corrected clustering score is read against the cluster sizes and against a supervised ceiling, computed with patient-grouped five-fold cross-validation on the same records and representation. A high ceiling with a low score then marks a failure of geometry, and a low ceiling marks missing information.
\item The internal silhouette index and the feature conditioner are tested as sources of misleading results, which explains the original project's numbers.
\item Hand-crafted features are compared with two named foundation models on PTB-XL. On a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, which the closest benchmark omits, they are compared with learned and raw representations.
\item Four of the representations are tested under two deployment stressors, heavy class imbalance and a simulated federation with skewed class proportions.
\end{enumerate}
Recent ECG foundation-model benchmarks compare models with one another or with supervised and random baselines (Section~\ref{sec:related}). This protocol adds a label-free reading against a hand-crafted baseline. We propose no new algorithm, we scope every foundation-model statement to the two models tested, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability effect to prior work.

\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Unsupervised ECG and representations.} Hand-crafted features built from RR-interval irregularity and beat morphology encode the defining signatures of common arrhythmias and underpin decades of automatic analysis \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}. He et al.~\cite{wavelettensorecg2018} combined wavelet tensor decomposition with spectral clustering for unsupervised 12-lead classification. A reconstruction autoencoder is trained to reproduce its input signal, so its training objective holds no class information. Contrastive self-supervised methods are built on physiologically motivated augmentations and patient- or lead-level invariances. They include CLOCS \cite{clocs2021}, 3KG \cite{threekg2021}, and contrastive predictive coding for 12-lead ECG \cite{mehari2022ssl}. Later foundation models reuse some of these objectives: ECG-FM adopts the multi-segment contrastive objective of CLOCS \cite{McKeen_2025}, and ECG-CPC is pretrained with contrastive predictive coding \cite{arxiv_2509_25095}. Self-supervised and foundation models for ECG, such as HuBERT-ECG \cite{hubertecg2024} and ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024,arxiv_2410_04133}, learn general representations from millions of recordings and are evaluated as frozen feature extractors. The tensor representation of He et al. addresses a related unsupervised goal, and we leave a tensor representation to future work. The five representations studied here range from raw samples, which keep only amplitude and energy, to features that measure the defining criteria of the classes.

\paragraph{Clustering evaluation.} Cluster purity is not chance-corrected: assigning
every point to the majority class attains a purity equal to that class's prevalence.
External, chance-corrected indices, the adjusted Rand index \cite{hubertarabie1985} and
adjusted mutual information \cite{vinh2010}, are near zero for such trivial solutions
and are the appropriate headline metrics. Internal indices such as the silhouette
\cite{rousseeuw1987} measure compactness in the embedding being clustered and can be
maximised by degenerate solutions, so they require cluster-size sanity checks.

\paragraph{ECG foundation-model benchmarks.} Several recent studies benchmark ECG foundation models on supervised downstream tasks. BenchECG, by Lunelli et al.~\cite{Lunelli_2026}, evaluates public models on eight datasets with 1\,674\,704 recordings. Al-Masud et al.~\cite{arxiv_2509_25095} compare eight foundation models on 26 tasks from 12 datasets, fine-tuned and frozen. Xu et al.~\cite{Xu_2025} compare language, general time-series and ECG foundation models with time-series deep-learning models. Berger et al.~\cite{arxiv_2602_17531} report that a randomly initialised encoder with linear evaluation matches pretrained encoders on many tasks. A review of open ECG foundation models names the absence of standardised evaluation frameworks as a challenge \cite{Han_2026}. None of BenchECG, Al-Masud et al. and Berger et al. evaluates clustering of the embeddings or includes a hand-crafted baseline.

\paragraph{Closest benchmark.} Filice et al.~\cite{lookingbeyond2026} evaluate ECG-FM, ECGFounder, HuBERT-ECG in three sizes and the ECG-JEPA of Weimann and Conrad as frozen feature extractors on four 12-lead datasets, PTB-XL among them. Beside classifier probes under class-balanced 15-fold cross-validation, they report the ARI of Gaussian-mixture clusters of the embeddings. They find that ECGFounder and ECG-FM separate their embeddings more by clinical label than by dataset, while HuBERT-ECG and ECG-JEPA separate them more by dataset than by label. Their benchmark compares foundation models with one another. It includes no hand-crafted baseline and does not use MIT-BIH. This study adds both. It also reads each clustering score against a supervised ceiling, computed with patient-grouped five-fold cross-validation on the same records and representation. A low clustering score can then be attributed to geometry or to missing information.

\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Data and cohorts}

\paragraph{PTB-XL.} We use PTB-XL, a public database of 10\,s 12-lead recordings, whose data descriptor reports 21\,837 records from 18\,885 patients \cite{ptbxl2020}. Its waveforms are stored in the WaveForm DataBase (WFDB) format as 16-bit samples at a resolution of \SI{1}{\micro\volt} per least significant bit. They are released at 500\,Hz and in a version downsampled to 100\,Hz \cite{ptbxl2020}. The benchmark uses the 100\,Hz version of release 1.0.3 \cite{ptbxl103}. Each record was read with the WFDB reader, which applies the conversion to physical units given in the record header. The signal was stored as a 32-bit floating-point array of 1000 samples by 12 leads. No other conversion precedes the representation steps of Section~\ref{sec:repr}.

Rhythm labels are taken from the statements of the Standard Communication Protocol for computer-assisted electrocardiography (SCP-ECG). The positives are drawn from the records carrying an AF statement, atrial fibrillation (AFIB) or atrial flutter (AFLT), and the controls from the sinus-rhythm records without one. PTB-XL encodes rhythm statements at a likelihood of zero meaning ``present'', so a presence-based rule is required, and a confidence threshold would discard every SR record. The records are taken in random order with one record per patient. The sinus-rhythm records come first, as many as there are patients with an AF record, followed by the AF records of patients not yet included. The resulting balanced, patient-disjoint cohort holds 2\,542 records (1\,282 SR, 1\,260 AF), so the majority-class baseline is 0.504. No patient appears in both classes, and the supervised ceiling uses patient-grouped folds. We study the binary SR-versus-AF task because one clinical criterion defines it. AF is recognised on the ECG by irregular RR intervals and the absence of discernible repeating P waves \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. With one defining criterion, the supervised ceiling and the chance-corrected score of Section~\ref{sec:clust} can be read against a known class difference.

\paragraph{MIT-BIH.} For generalization we use the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database \cite{mitbih2001,physionet2000}, whose two-lead records are sampled at 360\,Hz. The records were read with the WFDB reader into the physical signal and stored at 32-bit precision, in the same way as PTB-XL. Non-beat annotations were excluded. Each annotated beat is segmented into a window of $[-300, +250]$\,ms around the R peak across both leads. We form a balanced task of normal versus premature ventricular beats (N-versus-V). At most 400 beats per record and class were kept, and 1\,500 beats per class were then drawn at random, giving 3\,000 beats from 47 records. The record is the grouping unit. This task is defined by beat shape, and it is a deliberate contrast to the PTB-XL rhythm task.

\subsection{Representations}\label{sec:repr}

We compare five representations on PTB-XL. Table~\ref{tab:repr} gives the input, dimension and pretraining of each.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduces the representation of the original project. Each lead is averaged over non-overlapping windows of six samples, and the 12 leads are concatenated into one real-valued vector of about 2000 values. It keeps amplitude and waveform energy at fixed sample positions, with no alignment of beats across recordings.
\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE). The 12-lead strip is resampled to 2500 samples per lead and z-scored per lead. Three strided one-dimensional convolution layers, global average pooling and a linear layer map it to a 32-value code. The network is trained to reconstruct its input under the mean squared error, so the code keeps what reconstruction needs. This is a properly specified reconstruction model, in contrast to the collapsed dense autoencoder of the original project.
\item \textbf{Hand-crafted features} (16 values) are computed from lead II. The signal is cleaned, R peaks are detected and the waves are delineated with the NeuroKit2 toolbox \cite{neurokit2021}, and we selected its discrete wavelet method for the delineation. Eight features describe the RR intervals \cite{hrvstandards1996}. They are the mean RR interval, the standard deviation of the RR intervals (SDNN), the root mean square of successive differences (RMSSD) and the proportion of successive differences above 50\,ms (pNN50). The other four are the coefficient of variation of the RR intervals, the Shannon entropy of their histogram, the mean heart rate and the number of beats. Eight features describe the morphology \cite{dechazal2004}. They are the fraction of beats with a detected P wave, the mean and standard deviation of the P-wave amplitude, and the mean QRS width. The other four are the mean and standard deviation of the R-peak amplitude and of the correlation of each beat with the median beat. They encode the clinical criteria of rhythm regularity, P-wave presence and QRS shape. Missing values from a failed delineation are replaced by the column median.
\item \textbf{HuBERT-ECG} \cite{hubertecg2024}, a self-supervised transformer modelled on the HuBERT model for speech representation learning, receives the first 5\,s of the 12 leads at 100\,Hz. The leads are concatenated one after another into one sequence of 6000 samples, with no amplitude normalisation. The last hidden layer is averaged over time to 768 values.
\item \textbf{ECGFounder} \cite{ecgfounder2024,arxiv_2410_04133}, a convolutional network pretrained with supervision on diagnostic labels, receives the 10\,s 12-lead strip resampled from 100\,Hz to its 500\,Hz input (5000 samples per lead). The strip is z-scored with one mean and one standard deviation over all leads. The 1024 time-averaged features of the last convolutional stage are used, with the classification layer removed.
\end{itemize}
Both foundation models are used frozen, in evaluation mode, with no fine-tuning. Every representation is then transformed by the conditioner of Section~\ref{sec:clust} before clustering.

Only the hand-crafted features use a single lead. Raw flattening reproduces the 12-lead representation of the original project, and the conv-AE is given the same 12-lead strip. Both foundation models were pretrained on 12-lead input and are given the input they expect. We computed the hand-crafted features from a single lead because the defining signs of AF can be read in one lead. The guideline accepts as diagnostic a single-lead tracing of at least 30\,s that shows them \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. AF and other rhythm classes have also been classified from single-lead recordings \cite{clifford2017af}. We chose lead II as that lead. Zheng et al.~\cite{Zheng_2020} likewise extracted features such as the atrial rate and the mean and variance of the RR interval from lead II. The single-lead set leaves out inter-lead information, such as the electrical axis and lead-specific morphology, which other diagnoses need. In exchange, it can be computed from a single-lead recording. In this benchmark the single-lead features are compared with representations that see all 12 leads. The effect of adding leads to the hand-crafted set was not tested.

We chose the two foundation models by three criteria. Both have public weights and take 12-lead input. They differ in pretraining and architecture: HuBERT-ECG is a self-supervised transformer and ECGFounder a supervised convolutional network. Both are evaluated in the closest benchmark \cite{lookingbeyond2026}, which reports ECGFounder's embedding as organised by clinical label and HuBERT-ECG's as organised by dataset. We included ECGFounder for that report, so that the hand-crafted features are compared with a model reported to cluster by label.

On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not apply to single beats, so three beat representations are compared. The raw beat window flattens both leads over the $[-300, +250]$\,ms window into one vector of about 400 values. It keeps the amplitude at fixed positions around the R peak. Heartbeats of the MIT-BIH database have been classified from two-lead feature sets of morphology and RR intervals \cite{dechazal2004}. We defined 21 hand-crafted beat features in these two groups: five RR-context values and eight morphology values per lead. The RR-context values are the previous and next RR intervals, their ratio, the ratio of the previous interval to the local mean of up to six preceding intervals, and that local mean. They measure the prematurity of a beat. The morphology values are the R amplitude, the energy, the QRS width, the maximum, the minimum, the skewness and the kurtosis of the beat window. The eighth is the correlation of the window with the median of all 3\,000 beats of the task. They describe the shape of the beat. The conv-AE is applied to the beat window as on PTB-XL. The 550\,ms two-lead window is resampled to 2500 samples per lead and z-scored per lead before the same network, and the code again keeps what reconstruction needs. The foundation models were not applied to the two-lead beats, because their input is a 12-lead strip.

