\subsection{Clustering and evaluation}\label{sec:clust}

Each representation is clustered with five algorithms \cite{sklearn2011}: $k$-means, Ward agglomerative clustering (Ward), spectral clustering, a Gaussian mixture model (GMM) and hierarchical density-based spatial clustering of applications with noise (HDBSCAN) \cite{hdbscan2013}. For $k$-means, Ward, spectral clustering and the GMM the number of clusters is set to the number of classes. HDBSCAN is given no cluster count. It labels low-density points as noise and uses a minimum cluster size of the larger of 10 and one fiftieth of the number of records. The noise points keep their own label and are never reassigned to a cluster. For the ARI, the adjusted mutual information (AMI), the normalized mutual information (NMI), the V-measure and the Fowlkes-Mallows index (FMI), they therefore form one additional cluster. A solution that labels most points as noise then scores near zero. Purity counts noise points as misassigned. The silhouette is computed on the non-noise points. The largest-cluster fraction counts only non-noise clusters, over all records. No clusterer setting was tuned against the labels, and Table~\ref{tab:settings} gives the settings of every step. The default conditioner was chosen on a labelled pilot analysis, described below.

Table~\ref{tab:metrics} gives the meaning, formula and range of each metric. The headline metrics are the ARI \cite{hubertarabie1985} and the AMI \cite{vinh2010}, which are corrected for chance. The NMI \cite{Danon_2005}, the V-measure \cite{rosenberg2007vmeasure}, the FMI \cite{Fowlkes_1983}, purity, the silhouette \cite{rousseeuw1987} and the largest-cluster fraction are reported alongside. With the arithmetic-mean normalisation used here, the NMI equals the V-measure, so the two coincide in every result. The ARI is compared with a random labelling, drawn uniformly over the clusters, whose mean ARI over ten seeds is 0.000. Purity is compared with the majority-class proportion, 0.504.

A supervised ceiling measures how much of the label a classifier recovers from the same representation under the default conditioner. It is the accuracy of a balanced logistic regression under stratified, patient-grouped five-fold cross-validation, and its chance level is the majority-class proportion. A random forest on the same folds is reported as a non-linear check. A high ceiling with a low ARI then marks a failure of geometry, and a low ceiling marks missing information.

In the main benchmark each fit of $k$-means, spectral clustering and the GMM is repeated over ten seeds. The cohort, the raw and hand-crafted features, the two foundation-model embeddings and the trained conv-AE are fixed across seeds. The seeds change only the random initialisation of $k$-means, spectral clustering and the GMM. Ward and HDBSCAN are deterministic and are fitted once. The seed spread therefore measures the stability of the clustering step on a fixed cohort, and it does not measure the sampling uncertainty of the cohort. The headline contrasts are tested with a paired, two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the per-seed $k$-means ARI. With ten paired seeds the smallest attainable p-value is 0.002. A p-value at this floor gives the direction of a contrast and says nothing about its size. The difference in mean ARI is therefore reported as the effect size of each contrast.

Because heavy-tailed features can dominate Euclidean distance, four conditioners are swept: the Yeo-Johnson power transform, standard scaling, a quantile transform to a normal output and robust scaling. Each is fitted on the whole cohort. Power scaling is the default unless stated otherwise. We chose it after a pilot analysis on a smaller, labelled SR-versus-AF set, in which robust scaling collapsed the $k$-means partition of the hand-crafted features. Because this choice used the labels, all four conditioners are reported in the Results. We also report the AMI of $k$-means for $K$ from 2 to 8, with one fit per $K$. Because the AMI is comparable across cluster counts, this checks that fixing the cluster count to the number of classes does not flatter the comparison. The natural SR-to-AF prevalence is imbalanced, so we also evaluate a 9:1 SR:AF subsample. It holds all SR records and one ninth as many AF records, drawn once at random. It is conditioned on its own records and clustered once with $k$-means. The $K$ sweep, the random forest and the imbalance analysis cover raw flattening, the hand-crafted features and the two foundation models.

