\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/fig1_ari_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{PTB-XL SR-versus-AF clustering ARI by representation and clusterer under power scaling, on 2\,542 recordings (1\,282 SR, 1\,260 AF). Each $k$-means, spectral and GMM cell is the mean over ten seeds, and each Ward and HDBSCAN cell is one deterministic fit. The seed spread is given in Table~\ref{tab:main}. The axis labels kmean, agglo, spect, gmm and hdbsc stand for $k$-means, Ward, spectral clustering, the GMM and HDBSCAN, and raw-flatten stands for raw flattening.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

Across ten seeds the $k$-means solutions are stable. The seed standard deviation of their ARI is 0.000 for raw flattening, the hand-crafted features and HuBERT-ECG, and 0.001 for ECGFounder. Because the seeds vary only the initialisation, this spread measures the stability of the clustering step on a fixed cohort. A paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the $k$-means seeds compares the representations adjacent in the ranking of ECGFounder, the hand-crafted features, HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening. Each of the three contrasts gives $p = 0.002$, the floor for ten paired samples, and the conv-AE was not tested. The effect sizes are the differences in mean ARI on the chance-corrected scale. ECGFounder exceeds the hand-crafted features by 0.242, the hand-crafted features exceed HuBERT-ECG by 0.470, and HuBERT-ECG exceeds raw flattening by 0.174. The hand-crafted $k$-means partition has a purity of 0.901, against 0.506 for raw flattening and a majority-class proportion of 0.504. The ordering does not depend on fixing the cluster count to the number of classes. With the AMI for $K$ from 2 to 8, ECGFounder leads at every $K$ (0.83 falling to 0.45), and the hand-crafted features follow (0.56 to 0.33). HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening stay low (below 0.14 and near 0).

The two foundation models do not behave as one category. ECGFounder clusters strongly while HuBERT-ECG does not. This matches the report of Filice et al.~\cite{lookingbeyond2026} that some ECG foundation-model embeddings are organised by clinical label and others by dataset. We therefore name each model and do not generalise to foundation models as a class. HDBSCAN labelled every record as noise for raw flattening, the conv-AE and HuBERT-ECG, so these solutions form one cluster and score an ARI of 0. For the hand-crafted features and ECGFounder, its largest cluster held 0.140 and 0.202 of the records. A possible cause of the low spectral scores, such as 0.016 for HuBERT-ECG, is a poorly connected nearest-neighbour affinity graph, which was not checked here. We report both clusterers as fitted, without tuning them.

\subsection{Separability is not clusterability}

The supervised ceilings (last column of Table~\ref{tab:main}, Fig.~\ref{fig:sep}) expose the gap between separability and clusterability. The ceiling is an accuracy whose chance level is the majority-class proportion (0.504). The ARI is corrected for chance, with chance level 0. The two numbers are therefore read against their own chance levels and are not compared directly. Raw flattening carries almost no linearly decodable label information (ceiling 0.519), consistent with its chance-level clustering. The conv-AE is more separable (ceiling 0.678) yet clusters at chance (best ARI 0.008). HuBERT-ECG shows the largest gap between separability and clusterability, with a ceiling of 0.899 and a best ARI of 0.195. ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features achieve both high separability and high clusterability. We read the weaker representations as holding label information that does not lie along their dominant axes of variation. This reading is consistent with the gap between linear-probe accuracy and clustering quality reported for self-supervised image models \cite{sslclustering2025}. A non-linear probe confirms that label information can be present where clustering does not recover it. A random forest on raw flattening reaches an accuracy of 0.71, above its supervised ceiling of 0.519, while its clustering ARI is 0.

Each result has a likely explanation, and the study did not test these mechanisms directly. ECGFounder was pretrained with supervision on 150 label categories that include rhythm descriptions \cite{arxiv_2410_04133}. Its feature space is therefore likely organised by diagnosis, and Filice et al. report its embedding as aligned with the clinical label \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. The hand-crafted features measure the criteria that define AF, irregular RR intervals and absent P waves \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. The class difference therefore lies along the features' own axes, and their ceiling (0.942) and ARI (0.711) agree.

HuBERT-ECG was pretrained by self-supervision without diagnostic labels \cite{hubertecg2024}, and here it saw only the first 5\,s at 100\,Hz, averaged over time. Its ceiling of 0.899 shows that the label is present. A linear probe can use a direction of small variance that separates the classes, whereas $k$-means and the GMM partition along the directions of largest variance. In this embedding those directions appear to follow other structure than the rhythm class, consistent with the report that HuBERT-ECG's embedding is organised by dataset \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. The embedding thus holds the SR-versus-AF information, but recovering it in this setting needs labels. Whether the shorter, unfiltered input adds to the gap was not tested.

Raw flattening and the conv-AE score at chance for different reasons. With $k$-means, raw flattening places on average 0.967 of the records in one cluster, close to a majority-class assignment. The conv-AE forms two clusters of similar size (the larger holds 0.541 of the records at seed 0) that do not follow the rhythm class. Raw flattening compares samples at fixed positions without beat alignment, and the conv-AE is trained to reconstruct the waveform. Neither therefore places the rhythm difference along its dominant axes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/fig3_sep_vs_clust.pdf}
\caption{Separability and clusterability of the five PTB-XL representations, on the same 2\,542 recordings. Each point is one representation. The horizontal value is its supervised ceiling, the patient-grouped five-fold accuracy of a balanced logistic regression, with chance level 0.504. The vertical value is the mean ARI of its best clusterer, a mean over ten seeds or one fit for Ward and HDBSCAN, with chance level 0. The dashed line, labelled parity, joins equal values on the two axes and is drawn for orientation. Each coordinate is read against its own chance level, 0.504 for the ceiling and 0 for the ARI. Table~\ref{tab:main} gives every clusterer and the seed spread, and Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} shows one marker per fit. The point label raw-flatten stands for raw flattening.}
\label{fig:sep}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The silhouette trap}

Internal indices mislead here in a specific, reproducible way. Raw flattening with Ward attains a silhouette of 0.81 while all but a handful of points fall into one cluster (largest-cluster fraction 1.00) and the ARI is 0.000. The hand-crafted features, which recover the classes (ARI 0.711, balanced clusters), have a \emph{lower} silhouette of 0.28 (Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}). A degenerate one-cluster solution maximises compactness while carrying no class information. This is the artefact behind the original project's silhouette of 0.89. Chance-corrected external metrics, read together with cluster-size checks, are needed to detect it.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/fig2_silhouette_trap.pdf}
\caption{The silhouette trap on the 2\,542 PTB-XL recordings under power scaling. Each marker is one clustering fit: ten seeds for $k$-means, spectral clustering and the GMM, and one fit for Ward and HDBSCAN. The horizontal axis is the internal silhouette score and the vertical axis the chance-corrected ARI. Marker size is the largest-cluster fraction. Fits without a silhouette value, such as an HDBSCAN solution with every record labelled as noise, are not drawn. The degenerate one-cluster solutions (large markers, bottom right) attain a high silhouette at zero ARI. The legend label raw-flatten stands for raw flattening.}
\label{fig:trap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Conditioning matters}

The feature scaler changes the outcome materially. For the hand-crafted features the
mean ARI over clusterers is 0.63 under standard scaling and 0.61 under power scaling, but
falls to 0.36 under robust scaling and 0.23 under quantile scaling. The effect is not
confined to hand-crafted features: ECGFounder's mean ARI drops from 0.87 (standard) to
0.09 under robust scaling. Robust scaling, a common default for physiological signals,
leaves heavy arrhythmic tails unbounded so that a few outliers dominate Euclidean
distance and the partition collapses. Conditioning should be reported and swept, not
assumed.

\subsection{Generalization to MIT-BIH beat morphology}

The ordering replicates on a different dataset and a different task type (Table~\ref{tab:mitbih}). On the N-versus-V task the 21 hand-crafted beat features cluster well (ARI up to 0.783). The raw beat window does not (ARI at most 0.049), although the beats are aligned on the R peak. The conv-AE again separates without clustering: its supervised ceiling is 0.927, but its ARI is at most 0.127. The same pattern appears for rhythm on PTB-XL and for morphology on MIT-BIH, which supports the representation-level reading for these two tasks.

