\subsection{Class imbalance and simulated federation}

Two stressors closer to deployment change the comparison between ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features. Under class imbalance the hand-crafted features exceed ECGFounder, and under the simulated federation the two come close. The first stressor is class imbalance. At a 9:1 SR:AF ratio (majority 0.90), every representation's ARI falls. The hand-crafted features retain the most structure (ARI 0.232, AMI 0.214) and exceed ECGFounder (ARI 0.060, AMI 0.143), with HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening near zero. ECGFounder's lead on balanced data does not hold under this imbalance. This analysis rests on one random subsample and one $k$-means fit, so it has no seed spread.

The second stressor is the simulated federation (Table~\ref{tab:fed}, Fig.~\ref{fig:fed}). Under the IID split across ten sites, every representation keeps essentially its centralized ARI, which checks the federated procedure. The non-IID class-proportion skew lowers every non-zero result, but unequally. The hand-crafted features keep 0.92 of their centralized ARI (0.644 to 0.591, seed standard deviation 0.067). ECGFounder falls from 0.885 to 0.612 with a seed standard deviation of 0.350, so its federated result varies strongly across partitions and initialisations. Under this skew ECGFounder (0.612) and the hand-crafted features (0.591) come close. In both stress tests the hand-crafted features give the more stable result, within this simulation on one dataset.

