\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

The practical reading holds for the settings tested: two binary tasks, five clusterers, two named foundation models and a simulated federation. On balanced PTB-XL data in the 12-lead format, ECGFounder gave the best unsupervised SR-versus-AF separation. Under 9:1 class imbalance the hand-crafted features exceeded it, and under the simulated class-proportion skew the two came close. The hand-crafted features are also the lowest-dimensional representation (16 values against 768 and 1024, Table~\ref{tab:repr}) and are computed from one lead without a neural network. Both foundation models need a forward pass through a network of 76 to 93 million parameters. Run time and memory were not measured, so these properties indicate cost without quantifying it. We judge that the low dimension may also suit distance-based clustering, which this study did not test. The foundation models were not applied to the two-lead MIT-BIH beats, because their input is a 12-lead strip, so the study makes no comparison on other lead sets. Raw flattening and the conv-AE, including the original project's design, scored at chance on PTB-XL with all five clusterers. On MIT-BIH their ARI was at most 0.127.

The result also reframes the original master's project. Its conclusion that the bottleneck is the representation rather than the clustering algorithm holds in this benchmark, and we have quantified it. Its headline metrics were the artefacts of two avoidable choices, purity without a baseline and silhouette on a collapsed embedding. Both are corrected here.

We propose no new algorithm. The gap between linear-probe accuracy and clustering quality has been reported for self-supervised image models \cite{sslclustering2025}, the centroid averaging follows federated averaging \cite{fedavg2017}, and the representations are existing tools. The contribution is the evaluation protocol set out in the Introduction. It reads chance-corrected clustering scores against the cluster sizes and against a patient-grouped supervised ceiling, and it sweeps the conditioner. It compares a hand-crafted baseline with two foundation models on PTB-XL, and with learned and raw representations on a MIT-BIH beat task, and adds two stress tests. Recent benchmarks evaluate foundation models on supervised downstream tasks \cite{Lunelli_2026,arxiv_2509_25095,Xu_2025} or compare their embeddings with one another \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. The protocol here adds a label-free reading against a hand-crafted baseline. Other ECG foundation models with public weights include ST-MEM \cite{arxiv_2402_09450}, ECG-JEPA \cite{Kim_2027}, MERL \cite{arxiv_2403_06659}, ECGFM-KED \cite{Tian_2024,arxiv_2509_25095}, ECG-FM \cite{McKeen_2025} and ECG-CPC \cite{Lunelli_2026}. Recent benchmarks evaluate them with their public weights \cite{arxiv_2509_25095,Lunelli_2026}. Their training objectives differ, from masked reconstruction and joint-embedding prediction to contrastive alignment of signal and report text \cite{arxiv_2509_25095}. Every foundation-model statement here holds for HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder. The hand-crafted features were compared with a model reported to cluster by label \cite{lookingbeyond2026}, and a model trained with another objective could rank differently.

\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

ECGFounder expects 500\,Hz input. Because the 500\,Hz records could not be obtained in bulk from the source, the main benchmark embeds the 100\,Hz cohort upsampled to the expected length. A consistency check on a 300-record subset, rather than the full cohort, compares native 500\,Hz with upsampled 100\,Hz input. ECGFounder clusters almost identically in both (ARI 0.896 versus 0.909, a difference of 0.013). Upsampling adds no information, so it cannot inflate the foundation model's lead. The foundation-model inputs were also not filtered as their pretraining data were. HuBERT-ECG was pretrained on 5\,s inputs band-pass filtered to 0.05 to 47\,Hz and rescaled to $[-1, 1]$ \cite{hubertecg2024}, and ECGFounder on filtered inputs z-scored per segment \cite{arxiv_2410_04133}. The effect of matching this preprocessing was not tested. The foundation-model results cover two models with different pretraining. Other public models (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}), including some reported to cluster well \cite{lookingbeyond2026}, are not included, which is why we name the two we test.

Both tasks are binary, and the study makes no claim about multi-class rhythm structure. Public 12-lead databases label rhythms other than SR and AF \cite{Zheng_2020,Perez_Alday_2020,ptbxl2020}. Among such rhythms, RR irregularity alone would not single out AF, since non-AF rhythms can also show irregular RR intervals \cite{clifford2017af}. The MIT-BIH arm omits the foundation models because their input is a 12-lead strip. The federated study simulates class-proportion skew on a single dataset. It fits the conditioner and draws the initial centroids on the pooled records, which a real multi-site deployment could not do.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

For unsupervised SR-versus-AF and N-versus-V clustering on PTB-XL and MIT-BIH, the representation changed the result more than the choice among the clustering algorithms tested. Chance-corrected external evaluation was needed to see it. On balanced data ECGFounder led. With 9:1 class imbalance the 16 hand-crafted features exceeded it, and under simulated class-proportion skew they came close (0.591 against 0.612). Raw flattening and the conv-AE scored at chance on PTB-XL with every clusterer tested. Internal indices and feature conditioning changed the apparent result and should be reported. These conclusions hold for two binary tasks, two named foundation models and a simulated federation.

\section*{Code and data availability}
PTB-XL (version 1.0.3) \cite{ptbxl2020,ptbxl103} and the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database (version 1.0.0) \cite{mitbih2001} are public on PhysioNet. The benchmark code, with the exact cohort definitions, the settings of Table~\ref{tab:settings} and the list of software dependencies with their minimum versions, is available at \url{https://github.com/TheAxeC/ecg-representation-clustering}. The pretrained weights of HuBERT-ECG were obtained from \url{https://huggingface.co/Edoardo-BS/hubert-ecg-base} and those of ECGFounder from \url{https://huggingface.co/PKUDigitalHealth/ECGFounder}.

\section*{Ethics}
This study uses only de-identified, publicly available databases and required no new
human-subjects data collection.

